pra: per-sector CMD17 back as the default; CMD23+CMD18 opt-in; floods made impossible

0162 (auto CMD12) and 0162b (emmcmulti=2, explicit CMD12 stops) both
flooded "card busy, skip read" and never mounted the root. Diagnostic
verdict after two boots: the wedge is not in how the transfer is
stopped - it is that CMD18 without CMD23 is an open-ended stream. The
controller's BLOCK_COUNT register caps what the CONTROLLER accepts, the
card does not know it, and this Arasan never delivered any stop, so the
card streamed, held DAT, and every later command answered EBUSY until
the next power cycle. EBUSY was exempt from the three-strike latch, so
the skip loop never ended.

Ring21 evidence: the pull came back byte-identical to ring20 - old GPT
geometry (first=10715136), "CMD18 disabled by boot arg", zero card-busy
lines. Both flood boots lost ALL log evidence because the pralog
flusher writes through the same wedged strat path. After this patch no
boot can flood, so the ring always survives.

- Reads are per-sector CMD17 by default again, the exact IO path of
  every healthy boot (0156..0161d with emmcmulti=0); no boot-arg needed.
- emmcmulti=1 opts in to the CMD23 form: SET_BLOCK_COUNT tells the CARD
  to stop after exactly N blocks (extcsd rev 8 => MMC 5.x, CMD23 since
  eMMC 4.4); the auto-CMD12 bit stays clear and no stop is ever sent.
  Ladder unchanged: 2 -> 8 -> 16 -> 32 -> 64 -> 128 sectors, printed.
- emmcmulti=2 (manual stop) is removed: its diagnostic answer came back
  "still wedges".
- Any failed chunk now runs pra_emmc_recover() (CMD12 stop, CMD|DAT
  soft reset, ready wait) and counts toward the latch regardless of the
  error type - busy-after-a-chunk is the wedge signature, not
  transience. Two consecutive failures latch multi off for the boot.
- "card busy, skip read/write" prints are rate-limited to one line per
  10 s with a running count (also in the stats line as "busy-skip
  %llu"), so a future wedge cannot flood the screen or starve the
  flusher even before the latch fires.

